CCP_1167_TITLE = 'The absence-only scanner matches helpers by name, so 27 new subprocess tests never entered its corpus and its pin stayed silent'
CCP_1167_DESCRIPTION = (
    'FOUND 08.09.2026 (finding F8). RE-MEASURED and freshly REPRODUCED 09.09.2026 with a new instance.\n\nscripts/tests/test_absence_only_assertions.py exists to catch a test that starts a subprocess and then asserts only the ABSENCE of something -- an assertion that cannot fail. Its own regression pin (line 1866, `self.assertEqual(1371, len(recs))`) is the signal that the corpus changed shape: "A change in either number means a test changed shape or this scanner\'s own logic changed -- a deliberate look either way, never a silent drift."\n\nTHE BLIND SPOT is the helper\'s NAME, not its behaviour: line 185, `RUN_HELPER_RE = re.compile(r"^_?run(_\\w+)?$")`. A test method enters scope only when it calls something shaped like that. A helper doing exactly the same thing under any other name is invisible.\n\nFRESH INSTANCE, measured 09.09.2026 (CCP-1166, commit 6495771): 27 new test methods landed in scripts/tests/test_install_provenance.py. Every one of them starts install.sh in a subprocess. They call it through `self.verify(...)` and `self.plant(...)` -- 27 occurrences of `self.verify(` in the diff -- and NOT ONE matched RUN_HELPER_RE. The pin stayed at 1371 and the suite stayed green. The check that exists to notice a shape change did not notice 27 of them.\n\nNOT A NEW DISCOVERY TO THE FILE ITSELF: its own docstring already records the same shape for `self.push(...)` and for `self.popen_check_all(...)` ("the same naming-shape blind spot recorded below"). Those two were survivable because each affected method ALSO called a matching helper. The CCP-1166 methods do not, so this is the first instance where the blind spot removes methods from the corpus outright rather than merely under-attributing them.\n\nSCOPE QUESTION TO SETTLE FIRST: (a) widen RUN_HELPER_RE to a name list including the known cases, (b) stop matching on the NAME and detect the subprocess call inside the helper\'s body, or (c) require an explicit opt-in marker on subprocess helpers. (a) is cheapest and drifts again at the next helper; (b) is what the check actually means; (c) makes the corpus a hand-kept register, the shape G-091 warns about.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n  1. The 27 CCP-1166 methods are in scope, and the pin moves by exactly that measured amount -- not by an assumed one.\n  2. Red proof: a test method calling a subprocess helper under a non-`run` name, asserting only an absence, is FLAGGED. Silent counter-proof: the same method with a positive assertion is not.\n  3. The pin\'s own trajectory block records the new number with the reason, one line per event, as it already does.\n  4. Whatever the scanner ends up matching on is stated in the docstring together with what it does NOT reach.\n\nRELATED: CCP-1166 (the 27 methods that exposed it), CCP-1152 (derive counted facts rather than typing them).'
)

CCP_1136_TITLE = 'A number that is a property of the pattern or the probe, not of the subject — six instances, no mechanism'
CCP_1136_DESCRIPTION = (
    'PO observation 31.08.2026, after a fifth instance in one series. Recorded WITHOUT a solution direction: whether this becomes a tool or a rule is the PO\'s question.\n\nTHE CLASS. A measurement returns a number that is a property of the PATTERN or\nthe PROBE, not of the subject. It does not fail. It returns something\nplausible, and plausible is what gets believed and written down.\n\nSIX INSTANCES, all in one series, all found only because something else\nforced a re-measurement:\n\n1. The nine portability findings -- the sighting\'s breadth was the pattern\'s\n   breadth, not the corpus\'s.\n2. The catalogue number: 15 / 9 / 4 live sites, three answers to one question,\n   depending on whether path hits and derived (N-1) phrasings were counted.\n3. R5\'s reference surface: 74 files / ~1900 occurrences by one pattern,\n   79 / 961 by another. The difference was whether path-shaped hits counted.\n4. 31.08.2026, Manual/GETTING_STARTED.md: `grep -n \'eight phases\'` matched\n   NOTHING, and the finding was briefly reported as fixed. The sentence runs\n   across a line break. Over re.sub(r\'\\s+\', \' \', text) the count is 2, and the\n   second occurrence had never been recorded at all.\n5. 31.08.2026, building a set proof: a probe read `rec.method`; the attribute\n   is `method_name`. It did not raise -- getattr returned None for every\n   record, the set collapsed to unique filenames, and it reported a believable\n   "33 -> 34, one addition". The true figures were 1176 -> 1213, +37.\n\nWHY THIS ONE HAS NO PROCEDURE YET. Every other defect class in this repository\nhas acquired a mechanism -- absence-only assertions, register drift, live\nstatus claims, stored derivations (ADR-0012), exit status behind a pipe. This\none has only a habit: writing the method next to the number. A habit caught\nnone of the six; each was caught by an unrelated re-measurement.\n\nDELIBERATELY LEFT OPEN, NOT DECIDED: whether these are ONE class or TWO. The\nprose cases (1-4: line breaks, noun binding, word boundaries, path-shaped\nhits) fail at the corpus/pattern boundary. The probe cases (5, and arguably 2)\nfail inside the measuring code, where an attribute that does not exist is\nindistinguishable from one whose value is None. A misspelling would have\nraised; a wrong-but-absent field answers a different question quietly. Those\nmay want different answers.\n\nSIXTH INSTANCE, 01.09.2026 -- a tool that reports its own empty run, and would\nstill have passed as proof. Verifying a gate fix end to end, `memory-sync.sh\npromote` was run against a throwaway bare repository with an UNCHANGED source\nfile. It printed "gate clean", cloned, and exited 0 -- via the branch `no\nchange to promote (already up to date)`, which reaches `return 0` before\n`commit` and `push` are ever called. Read as "the promote path works again",\nthat run proves the gate and the clone, and nothing about the two steps the\nfix was supposed to restore. The exit code of a run that did the work and the\nexit code of a run that did nothing are identical.\n\nIt belongs to the probe half, but with a twist that makes it worse than case\n5: the tool did NOT fail quietly. It said, in plain words, that it had changed\nnothing. The number that was a property of the probe rather than the subject\nwas the exit code, and the honest message sat right above it. It was caught\nonly because the run was repeated with a genuinely modified file, and the\nsecond run\'s output looked different.\n\nThis is the same shape as "a check run that reports no scope is not a pass"\n(KA-G-017), moved one level up: there the scope was empty, here the ACTION was\nempty. The rule that would have caught it is not about the pattern at all --\nverify the STATE AFTER, not the exit code: the commit SHA in the remote, the\nline read back out of the pushed tree.\n\nMigrated from WI-0137.'
)

CCP_1124_TITLE = 'A test asserting only the absence of a finding cannot fail — flag it'
CCP_1124_DESCRIPTION = (
    'Filed 27.08.2026 on the PO\'s approval, from WI-0122\'s second round.\n\n**Correction (27.08.2026, PO-approved):** this item originally said "eight" below. That number is wrong — it counted the eight METHODS of `CheckHCoversTest` itself (WI-0122\'s commit message), not the negative-only ones among them. Measured directly (both by hand and independently by the scanner this item built, against the exact parent state of the fixing commit): **six** of `CheckHCoversTest`\'s and `CheckHCoversEmptyDirectoryDetectionTest`\'s 11 combined methods asserted only absence; the other five carry a positive assertion. File-wide, the same shape appears in **21 of 74** `test_phase_docs_lint.py` methods at that state — most of them in checks (a)-(g), never touched by WI-0122\'s by-hand fix.\n\n**The finding.** Six tests in `scripts/tests/test_phase_docs_lint.py`\'s `covers:` classes asserted only the absence of a finding:\n\n```python\nwarnings = self.findings(result.stdout, "Warnings")\nself.assertFalse(any("covers:" in w for w in warnings), warnings)\n```\n\nWhen the script died with empty stdout, `findings()` returned `[]` and every one of them passed. They could not distinguish "ran and correctly said nothing" from "did not run at all". A regression that killed the tool outright — exit 1, zero bytes, no report — was reported by the full suite as **1481 tests, OK**.\n\nThe fix in that work item was to add a liveness assertion (`files_scanned()`, which raises when the report line is missing) as the first assertion in each. Removing the guard afterwards turns five of them red. But that fix was applied by hand to the tests someone happened to look at.\n\n**The general rule** (G-126): an assertion of the form "X did not happen" is worth nothing without a paired assertion that the thing capable of producing X actually ran. Absence of a finding and absence of a run are the same observation.\n\n**What is proposed.** Make it a check rather than a memory: something that flags a test whose only assertions about a subprocess result are negative — `assertFalse(any(...))`, `assertEqual(len(...), 0)`, `assertNotIn(...)` — with no positive assertion about the run itself.\n\n**Design questions:**\n\n- *Scope.* This is a heuristic over test source, and heuristics over source produce false positives. It must be advisory (a warning naming the test) rather than an error that blocks, at least until measured — and the measurement is: how many of the existing ~1489 tests does it flag, and how many of those are genuinely blind?\n- *What counts as a liveness assertion?* `files_scanned()` in one file, a return-code check in another, an `assertIn` on a report header elsewhere. A rule that only recognises one project\'s helper is useless in another. Recognising "at least one positive assertion about the result object" is probably the honest bar.\n- *Where does it run?* If it is a lint over test files, it belongs beside the other lints, not inside the test suite — a test that fails because another test is weak is confusing.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n- Run against the state of `test_phase_docs_lint.py` **before** WI-0122\'s second round, it flags exactly the six blind `covers:` tests and none of the five sibling methods that carry a positive assertion. This is the discriminating case and the item is not done without it.\n- Run against the current tree, the flagged count is reported and each flagged test is triaged as genuinely blind or a false positive — silently tuning the rule until the count looks good is the failure mode here.\n- The rule recognises liveness in more than one shape.\n\nMigrated from WI-0125.'
)

